🚀 The feature, motivation and pitch
For each nn.Module that I define, I usually use a custom container type to group  its .forward input arguments and other related tensors and metadata, for instance
from dataclasses import dataclass
from torch import Tensor

@dataclass
class MyBatch:
    features: torch.Tensor
    sample_weights: torch.Tensor
    ...
in conjunction with a custom collate function returning MyBatch objects. With this setup, I always find myself implementing a thin wrapper around DataLoader to get more type checking especially when iterating on a DataLoader. The wrapper is the following:
from typing import Generic, TypeVar
from torch.utils.data.dataloader import DataLoader, T_co, _BaseDataLoaderIter

BatchT = TypeVar("BatchT")

class TypedDataLoaderIter(Generic[BatchT], _BaseDataLoaderIter):
    def __iter__(self) -> "TypedDataLoaderIter[BatchT]":
        return self

    def __next__(self) -> BatchT:
        return super().__next__()


class TypedDataLoader(DataLoader[T_co], Generic[T_co, BatchT]):
    def __iter__(self) -> TypedDataLoaderIter[BatchT]:
        return super().__iter__()
This allows more type information to flow through the code.
dataloader: TypedDataLoader[DatasetSample, MyBatch] = ...
for batch in dataloader:
    batch   # the type checker correctly types this as `MyBatch`

dataloader: DataLoader[DatasetSample] = ...
for batch in dataloader:
    batch   # the type checker gives no type here
I wondered if this was of any interest to add this type information natively to the DataLoader object.
